            <ArticleTitle>Progressive arm weakness and tonic hand spasm from multifocal motor neuropathy in the brachial plexus.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>242-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 50-year-old waitress presented with a 10-year history of progressive weakness in her right arm without atrophy and with tonic hand spasms suggesting a central motor disorder. Electromyography, however, disclosed chronic neurogenic changes including fasciculations and atypical cramps. Isolated motor conduction block in the right brachial plexus suggested a variant of multifocal motor neuropathy. Strength recovered and cramps disappeared after intravenous immunoglobulins. Motor neuropathies may thus manifest with features of central motor disorders.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
